Black bear
= bear
---
Five toes and a wide pad, like a small flat human foot with claws. Front and hind prints differ in length.

@img images/coyote.jpg
---
Coyote
---
Four toes, claws showing, oval and neat: X-shaped negative space between pad and toes. Domestic dogs splay wider and walk sloppier lines.

@img images/red-fox.jpg
---
Red fox
= fox
---
Like a coyote's but daintier, and the small pad often shows a chevron-shaped bar. Fur can blur the whole print.

@img images/cougar.jpg
---
Cougar
= mountain lion
= puma
---
Four toes, no claws (retracted), big round print with a three-lobed heel pad. Claw marks would mean canine.

@img images/canada-lynx.jpg
---
Canada lynx
= lynx
---
Cat print but huge for the body: furred paws act as snowshoes, so edges look soft and undefined.

@img images/raccoon.jpg
---
Raccoon
---
Five long finger-like toes, like a tiny human hand. Front and hind often land side by side.

@img images/moose.jpg
---
Moose
---
Two-toed heart shape like a deer's but much larger: over 12 cm long, and dewclaws print in deep snow or mud.

@img images/white-tailed-deer.jpg
---
White-tailed deer
= deer
---
Two-toed heart shape, points aim in the direction of travel. Moose is the same shape twice the size.

@img images/red-squirrel.jpg
---
Red squirrel
= squirrel
---
Paired bounding pattern: larger hind feet land ahead of the small front feet.

@img images/wild-turkey.jpg
---
Wild turkey
= turkey
---
Three long forward toes, the middle longest, up to 10 cm — far bigger than any songbird.

@img images/striped-skunk.png
---
Striped skunk
= skunk
---
Five toes on all feet with long front claw marks from digging; flat-footed, unhurried gait.

@img images/canada-goose.jpg
---
Canada goose
= goose
---
Webbing between three thick toes, prints toed inward.
